  The service is within ±8% of the reference cache per request on both, and misses per request match (1,683 vs 1,682 on set A). The
  whole 1.4–1.6x gap is 1.45–1.48x more lookups per request, which is access shape and off-limits under identical request logs. Profile
  shares match too: hashing is 66% of our lookup thread and 67% of the reference's on set B, so per-request work is at parity too.
  The 15% latency target is unreachable, and the July report's own section 25.2 (2026-08-08), which I missed when proposing, had already
  closed hash micro-optimisation after a 38-run study: fast path at 20 instructions vs the reference's 18, mostly memory-bound, "perfect
  parity buys under 3%". No commits were made; the branch cache-per-request-cost is empty. The baseline build finished in 5m48s at
  ~/work/targets/base/release/service in the unverified lane, since the verified lane refuses this repo's release config.
  Two plan defects surfaced by the workflow's independent proposer, both confirmed: the two sample traces never go quiet in 5 minutes,
  so they cannot serve the counter gate; and set D runs about 6 hours, not 20 minutes, so wall replicates
  should use sets C and E.
  Amended lever the proposer found, which I checked in code and endorse: the shipped posture (--audit … --audit-format json)
  runs a different and worse eviction than --fast because several gates key on audit_log.is_some() without an auditing
  reason. Measured on set A today: misses 357,574 → 385,057 (+7.7%) and lookups +6% under the audit log. Verified sites: a
  ghost-key entry is dropped under audit (evict.rs:1136), prefetch's warm entries are skipped under
  audit (prefetch.rs:94), the compaction budget is a wall clock scaled 4x under audit (constants.rs:1055, also a load-dependent
  nondeterminism hazard), and the sweeper never logs, so it never runs in the shipped configuration (capability.rs:135). Gate:
  audit and plain arms must produce identical misses, evictions, lookups and ticks on sets A, B, C and E; the log checker
  must verify the emitted log; the log must be byte-deterministic across two runs. This is not identical to today's shipped eviction
  by design, so it needs your call. The three refuters of the original lever are still running and I will fold in anything material when
  they land.
  Waiting for your go on the amended lever, or a different direction.
